- Media Gallery Plugin for Geeklog
- development version: 1.8.0
- branch: modernize-1.8.0
- original author: Yoshinori Tahara - dengen <taharaxp AT gmail DOT com>
- license: GPLv2
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

MediaGallery 1.8.0 is currently under development.

This branch modernizes the plugin while preserving compatibility with existing
MediaGallery 1.7.x installations. The work is intentionally split into a
planning/documentation phase followed by implementation and regression testing.

See ROADMAP.md for the detailed 1.8.0 plan.

Overview
--------
The Geeklog Media Gallery Plugin (formerly called Media Gallery WKZ) is an
integration of Media Gallery v1.6.1 Japanese version and Media Gallery v1.6.8
from glFusion CMS, subsequently adapted and maintained for Geeklog.

MediaGallery provides album and media management, thumbnails, image and video
handling, comments, RSS, search integration, member albums, permissions,
autotags and administrative media tools.

MediaGallery 1.8.0 goals
------------------------
The 1.8.0 release focuses on the following areas:

* safer upgrade from MediaGallery 1.7.x;
* multisite-aware media storage without requiring hostname-specific plugin code;
* cleanup and migration of remaining hard-coded plugin preferences to the
  Geeklog Configuration API;
* removal or replacement of obsolete playback and JavaScript technologies;
* modernization of .thtml templates, markup and responsive behavior;
* accessibility and SEO improvements where MediaGallery generates indexable
  public pages;
* better PHP 8.x compatibility;
* modernization of email notifications using Geeklog-supported templates;
* a public MediaGallery service API for album discovery by other plugins;
* preservation of existing permissions and media data.

Compatibility policy
--------------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 must remain safe for normal single-site Geeklog installations.

A standard installation that does not define any multisite-specific image URL
must continue to use the historical storage location:

    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/

with the public URL:

    {site_url}/mediagallery/mediaobjects

The upgrade process must not automatically move media files.

Multisite support
-----------------
Geeklog already exposes the physical site image path through:

    $_CONF['path_images']

MediaGallery 1.8.0 can additionally use an optional site-specific public image
URL defined by the site configuration:

    $_CONF['images_url'] = $_CONF['site_url'] . '/images/SITE';

When both `path_images` and `images_url` are available, MediaGallery derives the
site-specific media location from them instead of relying on HTTP_HOST checks.

Example:

    $_CONF['path_images'] = $_CONF['path'] . 'public_html/images/example/';
    $_CONF['images_url']  = $_CONF['site_url'] . '/images/example';

MediaGallery then resolves its media location as:

    .../public_html/images/example/mediagallery/

and:

    https://example.com/images/example/mediagallery

This mechanism is optional. Sites without `images_url` keep the legacy
MediaGallery path unchanged.

Configuration modernization
---------------------------
Older MediaGallery versions exposed many settings in a manually edited
`config.php`. Current releases no longer ship that file and already use the
Geeklog Configuration API for many options.

Version 1.8.0 will complete that migration where appropriate.

Administrator preferences still hard-coded in `functions.inc` will be reviewed
and moved to the online Geeklog configuration when they are still relevant.
Derived paths, plugin metadata, table mappings, media constants and other
internal values will remain code-controlled.

Obsolete settings will not be restored merely for historical compatibility.
Options tied only to retired technologies such as Flash, QuickTime-specific
playback, Windows Media Player, MooTools or SimpleViewer will be removed when
no supported code path still needs them.

Existing administrator configuration values must never be reset during a
1.8.0 upgrade. New configuration entries must be added only when missing.

Templates, accessibility and SEO
--------------------------------
The 1.8.0 work includes an audit of MediaGallery .thtml templates and frontend
output.

The modernization goals are:

* use current HTML5 markup;
* remove presentation-only table layouts where practical;
* improve responsive behavior;
* reduce obsolete or duplicated JavaScript dependencies;
* preserve theme/template overrides;
* improve form labels and keyboard accessibility;
* improve heading hierarchy and alternative text;
* review album and media page titles, metadata, canonical behavior and
  pagination for search engines;
* preserve and verify XML Sitemap integration;
* avoid duplicate structured data when Geeklog core or the active theme
  already provides it.

SEO changes must only use metadata actually available from MediaGallery and
must not invent descriptions or structured data.

Plugin interoperability
-----------------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 plans to expose a public service for retrieving visible album
trees through the Geeklog plugin service mechanism. This will allow plugins
such as Documents and future integrations to use MediaGallery without querying
its internal tables directly.

The target service concept is documented in ROADMAP.md.

Email modernization
-------------------
MediaGallery notifications will be reviewed so messages can use the current
Geeklog email template mechanisms, including plaintext and HTML variants where
supported, instead of embedding presentation directly in PHP.

Upgrade notes
-------------
Before any upgrade, back up both the MediaGallery database tables and media
files.

Media files in traditional MediaGallery installations are normally stored in:

    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/covers
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/disp
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/orig
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/tn

During development of 1.8.0 these existing locations must remain valid unless
an administrator explicitly configures a site-specific image location.

Do not manually move media files merely to test the 1.8.0 branch.

Current stable baseline
-----------------------
The 1.8.0 development branch is based on MediaGallery 1.7.3.

Important fixes already present in that baseline include:

* remote media upload fixes;
* PHP 7 and PHP 8.1 compatibility fixes;
* permission fixes for comments;
* Geeklog 2.2.2 compatibility work;
* XML Sitemap integration fixes;
* fixes for thumbnail filenames containing multiple periods;
* fixes related to upload size handling and other 1.7.x maintenance work.

These fixes must be retained in 1.8.0.

Installation and upgrade documentation
--------------------------------------
See docs/INSTALL for the historical installation and upgrade instructions.
These documents will be reviewed and updated before the 1.8.0 release candidate.

Development workflow
--------------------
The `modernize-1.8.0` branch is the working branch for this release.

Implementation order is defined in ROADMAP.md. In summary:

1. configuration and path refactoring;
2. multisite-safe storage handling;
3. Configuration API migration;
4. removal of confirmed obsolete code paths;
5. template and frontend modernization;
6. SEO and accessibility review;
7. public album service API;
8. email template modernization;
9. PHP/Geeklog compatibility and regression testing;
10. release documentation and 1.8.0 release candidate.

Change Log for Version 1.7.3
----------------------------
* Fixed remote upload of media issue.
* Fixes for PHP 7 and PHP 8.1.
* Fix for comments not taking into account user permissions on item.

Historical notes
----------------
MediaGallery has evolved through several generations of Geeklog and glFusion
integration. Older documentation and changelog material remains under `docs/`
and `docs/older/` for reference.

Some historical documentation describes technologies and configuration methods
that are no longer appropriate for 1.8.0. The 1.8.0 roadmap intentionally
reviews those areas instead of carrying them forward automatically.
